=== 1793660067005

  Earth turns through these bulges once a day, so most coastlines get two high tides and two low tides. The
  Moon also moves along its orbit as Earth spins, so a full tidal cycle takes about 24 hours and 50 minutes.
  That's why high tide comes roughly 50 minutes later each day. The Sun causes tides too, but its effect is a
  bit less than half the Moon's. When the Sun, Moon and Earth line up at new and full moon, their pulls combine
  into extra-large spring tides. When they sit at right angles, during the quarter moons, they partly cancel
  and give milder neap tides.
  Real tides are messier than this simple picture. The shape of coastlines, ocean basins and the seafloor can
  slow tides down, amplify them or redirect them. Some places get only one high tide a day, and Canada's Bay of
  Fundy has tides over 15 meters high.
